 &nbsp; 
 
 Cleaning out the notebook as we await Anthropic's first full day of trading... 

 ⏱️ Lag defense: On Monday, thousands of U.S. banking customers had difficulty accessing their accounts and making transactions because of a technical issue that began at JPMorgan Chase.

 What if something like this (or more widespread) had happened a few days later and Anthropic IPO investors weren't able to pay for their allocated shares? I asked some investment bankers about it, and they all admitted it was a problem they hadn't previously considered. Some argued that the process could be saved by Wall Street's one-day settlement period (T+1) — obviously depending on the payment glitch specifics — and wondered if this would reduce enthusiasm for T+0. That said, there also was a sense that Anthropic may still have paused its process to avoid the flood of legal work if share prices declined and investors tried to cancel their contracts before transfers went through. 🥇 Largest IPO ever: Anthropic officially broke the IPO record briefly held by SpaceX, after underwriters exercised greenshoe options to purchase more shares. The total raise comes out to $93.54 billion, not including the $26 billion concurrent private placement.

 🚪 Behind the scenes: I'm told that the Claude maker never seriously considered the SpaceX strategy of picking a set share price and sticking with it, instead wanting the investor feedback and price discovery that comes via the classic bookbuilding process.

 It also got a big confidence boost very early in the process, when large mutual funds put in allocation requests on Day 1. That's common for hedge funds that are seeking to develop a relationship with company management, but not for mutual funds that typically view themselves as having leverage. 💵 Price talk: There's an argument to be made that Anthropic did its IPO at a Goldilocks price. Enough of a pop that IPO buyers are in the black, but not so much that the company (and its bankers) look like they left a fortune on the table. 

 The counter, of course, is the headline risk tied to shares closing below the first trades and opening down another 9% this morning. Yes, IPOs are just a moment in time. But declining share prices can make it harder to hire and hurt internal morale (just ask anyone at Facebook post-IPO). For Anthropic, there's also all the counterfactuals about what would have happened had it not priced into an absolutely miserable trading day. And also questions about whether some of the declines were caused by rotation into Anthropic, although the declines likely were too broad to conclude causation. ⚡ Permit pass: The Senate last night passed the bipartisan permitting reform bill known as BAAJA. It now heads to the House, where Speaker Mike Johnson says it has enough votes to pass. 

 Axios' Ben Geman writes: "The eyes of the entire energy industry are on this bill. If it becomes law, it could transform how America builds energy infrastructure, with major implications for everything from electricity costs to data centers." ❓ Quiz time: Can you name the PE big whose departure had less to do with spending more time with family than the time he was spending with a subordinate? Hint: This also became an issue for some of the firm's LPs.

 📺 ICYMI: We've posted full videos of Axios BFD conversations with Vista Equity's Robert Smith , the NY Fed's John C. Williams , Goldman Sachs' Kim Posnett , baseball legend Alex Rodriguez , Lowercarbon Capital's Chris Sacca , Kalshi's Tarek Mansour , Capital One's Lia Dean with Maria Sharapova , Blackstone's Joan Solotar , and 1789 Capital's Omeed Malik &amp; Donald Trump Jr. 

 🌎 Where in the world: I'll be at the Boston Public Library at 12:30pm today to chat biz news with Jim and Margery on Boston Public Radio. Couches, coffee and conversation. Stop by if in the neighborhood.

 
 
